{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793342746,"nonce":"LQy67dH+ZZ6YW+kdw4Ub6ptTIcQ=","title":"Audit and align Rayzor AOT with Ash's recent compile-time improvements","message":"Compare Ash 909f1977 (AOT bypasses JIT promotion budgets), 7e105d9e (optimizing LLVM WASM shard cost), ff20945c (independent AIR/LLVM/codegen controls), and the bounded AIR preparation/cache changes.\n\nRayzor native AOT already declares all MIR functions, emits each body once via compile_module_bodies, and runs LLVM's module pipeline once. It does not call TieredBackend promotion or introduce pending-callee budget trampolines. Its WASM backend directly emits wasm-encoder instructions rather than using LLVM's WASM sharding pipeline, so that body-weight change has no matching stage to port.\n\nApplicable gaps: MIR/LLVM/machine-codegen optimization efforts are coupled, and native AOT unnecessarily clones all MIR modules after lowering despite an existing move-out API. Add independent MIR/codegen overrides with unchanged defaults to the CLI and library, keep mandatory MIR passes at O0, and validate optimized IR plus lower-effort machine code, cross-module/closure/virtual-call correctness, and the AOT path's independence from JIT policy. Run builds/tests and interleaved cold/warm measurements on an isolated NUC checkout, preserving the existing dirty worktrees. Record cache and profiling limitations honestly; do not equate BLADE's lowered MIR cache with Ash's optimized AIR body cache.","files":null}]}